{"id":"d0e6a38e-6dd4-44b3-a37c-364bd1ae472f","object":"chat.completion","created":1784988905,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    protected = {node['id'] for node in nodes \\n                 if node['id'] == 'root' or node['symbol'] in required_symbols}\\n    removed_order = []\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in removed_order:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable'] or nid in protected:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        temp_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(node_by_id[rid]['symbol'] == sym for rid in temp_retained):\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if ok:\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n            retained.remove(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The task: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" - reduce a parse tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols and some notion of interestingness. The payload contains nodes with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list.\n\nWe need to decide which nodes to remove, returning removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. The mechanism: consider deeper nodes first, never remove root, required symbol nodes, or nodes with retained children? Actually: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" And \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So it's like we try to prune nodes that are removable, but must ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., there exists at least one node with that symbol). Also we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained (i.e., if we plan to keep some descendant, we cannot remove the ancestor). But the order: deeper first.\n\nWe need to return removed_node_ids in order of removal (actual removal order). And retained_node_ids sorted (alphabetically? likely, but not specified; \"sorted retained IDs\" - probably sorted lexicographically). The output schema requires arrays of strings.\n\nWe need to parse case, which is the payload object. No other fields. We'll implement a function that processes the tree.\n\nProcedure: Build a tree from nodes list. Each node has parent id (null for root). We can build adjacency: children list. Also map id->node.\n\nWe need to determine which nodes are removable (removable: true). Root is always not removable. Nodes with symbol in required_symbols are not removable? The spec: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So required symbol nodes (i.e., nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols) cannot be removed even if removable flag is true? The spec says \"never remove ... a required symbol\". That likely means if the node's symbol is one of the required symbols, it cannot be removed. So we treat them as not removable for the algorithm.\n\nWe need to process nodes in order of depth descending (deeper first). For equal depth, use node ID as tie-breaker (stable). \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort nodes by (-depth, id) perhaps.\n\nWe iterate through nodes in that order. For each node that is removable (and not root, not required symbol), tentatively try to remove it. Check if after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., there exists at least one node (retained) with that symbol). But also we need to consider that we might remove ancestors later? Since we go deeper first, removal of a node might affect the condition for its ancestors? The rule \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Means after removing this node, the set of symbols from remaining nodes must still cover required_symbols. That is independent of parent-child relationships? Actually, if we remove a node, its descendants are also removed if they are not retained separately? But if we remove a parent, the children become orphaned? The spec says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means we cannot remove a node if any of its descendants (children) are retained (i.e., not removed). So we must only remove a node if none of its children are retained. Since we process deeper first, children are processed before parent. So if a child is retained (not removable or we decide to keep it), then parent cannot be removed.\n\nThus algorithm: we start with all nodes as potentially retained. We will iterate through nodes in order (deepest first). For each node that is removable (by flag and not required symbol, not root), we check if all its descendants that are still marked retained are none? Actually, \"node with retained children\" - if any child (or descendant?) is retained, you cannot remove the node. Since we process deeper first, we can mark nodes as removed when we decide. For a node, we need to know if any of its children are still retained (i.e., not yet removed). Since we go deeper first, children will have been processed before parent. So if a child was removed, it's fine. But if a child was not removed (e.g., because it's not removable or we decided to keep it), then parent cannot be removed.\n\nAlso, after removal, we need to ensure that required symbols are still covered. But if the node's symbol is not required, that's fine as long as other nodes with that symbol exist. However, we must check coverage after removal.\n\nWe need to decide whether to remove a node if conditions are met. It says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we attempt removal if possible. The order is removal order. So we need to simulate removals.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Create dict node_by_id from nodes list.\n2. Build children lists for each parent.\n3. Determine root (parent None).\n4. Identify nodes with symbol in required_symbols as protected (cannot be removed).\n5. Initialize set of removed_ids = empty.\n6. Initialize set of retained_ids = all ids initially.\n7. Create list of nodes to consider sorted: sort nodes by (-depth, id) (desc depth, then id ascending? \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for equal depth, order by id (string) ascending is stable. We'll sort by (depth descending, id ascending).\n8. For each node in that order:\n   - If node.id in removed_ids, skip.\n   - If not removable (removable flag false or protected root or protected symbol), skip (cannot remove).\n   - Check if node has any children that are still retained: i.e., any child id in retained_ids (since children might be removed already). If yes, skip (has retained children).\n   - Check if after removing this node, all required symbols still have at least one node in retained_ids (excluding this node). Create temporary set of retained after removal = retained_ids - {node.id}. For each required symbol, check if any node not removed has that symbol. If yes, we can remove.\n   - If both conditions met, remove node: add to removed_ids, remove from retained_ids, and record removal order (append to removed_node_ids list). Also, the node's descendants are automatically removed because we will never consider them again? Actually, descendants might have already been processed (since deeper first). But some descendants might not have been processed if they are shallower? No, all descendants are deeper, so they are processed before parent. If they were not removed, they are retained. But if parent is removed, those children become orphans? The spec says never remove a node with retained children. So if parent is removed, its children must have been removed already or not exist? Actually, we only allow removal if no retained children. So if a child was retained (i.e., we did not remove it), then parent cannot be removed. So if we remove parent, it means all its children were removed earlier. So no issue. Thus no need to mark descendants as removed; they already are.\n\n9. After processing all nodes, the retained_ids is the final retained set. Sort retained_ids alphabetically (string sort). Also, removed_node_ids is the order of removals.\n\n10. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained_ids}.\n\nEdge cases: root is never removable (removable false). Required symbol nodes: we should treat as not removable even if removable flag true? The spec says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So required symbol nodes are explicitly forbidden. So we should not attempt to remove them.\n\nAlso, the \"removable\" flag indicates if the node is a candidate for removal, but the above constraints override.\n\nCheck example: In first fixture, nodes: root depth0, n1 depth1 symbol s1 (required), n2 removable true, n3 depth1 symbol s0 (required? required symbols: root,s1 so s0 not required), n4 child of n2 removable true, n5 child of n3, n6 child of n1. Let's simulate: order by depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. n4 removable true, not required, children? none. Check after removal: required symbols: root and s1. root present, s1 present via n1 or n4? n4 has symbol s1, but after removal n4 is gone, but n1 still has s1. So OK. Remove n4. Then n5: not removable (false), skip. n6: removable true, symbol s0 not required, children none. After removing n6, required symbols still present (root, s1 via n1). So remove n6. Now depth1 nodes: n1 (removable false), n2 (removable true), n3 (removable false). n2: has children? n4 was removed, so no retained children. After removing n2, required symbols still have root and s1 (n1). So remove n2. n1 and n3 cannot be removed. root cannot. So removed: n4, n6, n2. retained: root, n1, n3, n5. That matches expected? Let's check expected from fixtures? Not given, but plausible. We should verify with the algorithm.\n\nNow implement in Python. Must return JSON-serializable (dict). Use only standard library.\n\nEdge: The case payload may have nodes list. We'll assume it's present.\n\nWrite solve(case) function.\n\nLet's produce the implementation code as a string for JSON submission."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2085,"completion_tokens":2445,"total_tokens":4530,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2068},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":37},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}